# SHA-256 of the sorted, normalized trueos_cabi function contracts declared in
# crates/trueos-v/src/bp_abi.rs for portal ABI epoch v2.
#
# Epoch v2 intentionally removes the synchronous Blueprint filesystem surface
# and introduces the operation-based async filesystem contract. Keep v1 as the
# historical baseline; incompatible pre-v2 Blueprints are rejected at prebind.
# The UI4 scene pan-event take, focused keyboard-state, and immutable-frame-open
# calls plus async filesystem write, mkdir, stat, and directory-list calls are
# strictly additive v2 extensions; older v2 artifacts do not import them and
# remain compatible. The vGPU vVideoMem create/cache-maintenance operations
# remain additive. The unsupported SceneDB AABB dispatch was intentionally
# removed from both kernel and Blueprint surfaces; artifacts importing it are
# rejected. The vGPU diagnostics record reports zero-copy counters and opaque
# mapping proof data.
# The UI4 retained-sprite upload, GPU-cleared sprite-frame begin, and ordered
# sprite-quad submission calls are strictly additive; existing v2 artifacts do
# not import them and retain their previous frame-write behavior.
# The UI4 resize-event take call is also strictly additive. It grants new
# Blueprints the existing resize-on-maximize notification while older v2
# artifacts continue to retain their fixed backing extent.
# The UI4 first-presentation take call is strictly additive. It exposes a
# per-window one-shot SURFLIVE acknowledgment without consuming the existing
# kernel-wide Spirit notification queue or changing older frame lifecycles.
# The UI4 output-dimensions query is strictly additive and read-only. It lets
# new Blueprints place scene frames relative to the same live pixel extent used
# by UI4 and mediated cursors; older v2 artifacts retain fixed coordinates.
# The UI4 input-routes snapshot is strictly additive and window-scoped. It
# exposes per-cursor selection, combo identity/color, and the already-sanitized
# paired held-key state without changing the singular legacy keyboard query.
# The UI4 custom-cursor declaration is strictly additive and lets a Blueprint
# suppress the default cursor over its selected frame. The cursor-icon call is
# likewise additive and selects built-in defaults per frame or cursor source.
# The pointer-event take call additively exposes only broker-routed events for
# the selected frame; it does not alter older consumers of the raw HID ABI.
# ParticleCraft render is likewise strictly additive. It accepts one versioned,
# pointer-free 64-byte control block; retained state and GPU addresses stay
# kernel-owned and existing UI4 producers retain their prior frame lifecycle.
# UI4 visual-frame open and ShaderToy render are strictly additive. Visual open
# adds a kernel-enforced 60 Hz ceiling to GPU-only dirty/double frames, while
# ShaderToy render accepts only a versioned pointer-free 64-byte uniform block
# and an immutable reviewed catalog id; executable bytes and GPU addresses stay
# kernel-owned.
# The visual-frame begin call is strictly additive. It keeps one visual
# admission request pending in the kernel until the brokered deadline and
# acquires only the kernel-owned GPU back buffer; older visual producers retain
# the nonblocking sprite-frame begin contract.
# The capability-backed mouse-motion, keyboard-control, and gamepad-control
# calls are strictly additive. They expose mediated high-level programs without
# changing the existing raw HID or selected-frame input contracts.
# VLayer InputCombo is the canonical high-level collection contract. The old
# narrowly named HUT combo snapshot is intentionally aligned to the complete
# TrueosInputCombo record so mouse, keyboard, tablet, and gamepad endpoints
# share identity/color metadata without a parallel versioned record or duplicate
# public hid_hut_*combo facade.
# The GridPaper sized-snapshot calls are strictly additive. They carry a
# bounded logical extent beside the unchanged fixed-capacity page image;
# existing full-size snapshot symbols and packaged Blueprints are unchanged.
# The GridPaper snapshot-checkpoint calls are strictly additive. They let a
# replicatable producer atomically capture kernel-mediated cell edits and
# release its disposable UI4/GPU projection; older producers keep their prior
# behavior.
# The lifecycle poll, Ready boundary, and identity calls are strictly additive.
# Old artifacts remain non-participants; tagged production artifacts can now
# quiesce before the host freezes them and observe identity after Resume.
# The Lumen session calls are strictly additive. They transfer prompts, replies,
# and a pointer-free mutable inference checkpoint while immutable model/GPU
# resources remain kernel capabilities. Spirit emotion playback is likewise an
# additive, idea-only capability used by the Blueprint-owned tool adapter.
# Spirit response presentation is strictly additive. It accepts only the
# Blueprint adapter's display-safe text after tool-control spans are handled;
# existing Lumen and non-Lumen Blueprints do not import it and remain unchanged.
# Silent Spirit text presentation is strictly additive. It queues validated
# display-safe text without requiring a Lumen session or entering a text-to-voice
# path; existing response-presentation behavior and imports remain unchanged.
# Spirit movement is strictly additive. It accepts only finite normalized
# scanout coordinates and queues them through Spirit's existing bounded,
# latest-wins movement capability; older Blueprints import no such authority.
# The archive operation calls are strictly additive. They expose owner-scoped,
# bounded, asynchronous TRUEOSFS path operations; older Blueprints do not
# import them, and no existing filesystem or codec contract changes.
# The UI4 font-canvas call is strictly additive. It accepts bounded colored
# rows and builds one window-retained premultiplied RGBA8 canvas without
# changing the legacy text-scene or frame-publish contracts.
# The versioned Shell2 frontend attach/read/input/detach calls are strictly
# additive. Existing v2 artifacts import none of them and retain their prior
# shell attachment and raw-write behavior.
# The UI4 keyboard-event take call is strictly additive. It exposes the input
# broker's existing owner/window route and leaves global keyboard-ring readers
# and older Blueprint input contracts unchanged.
# The Dobby UI4 inventory, focus, observation, pointer, and keyboard calls are
# strictly additive and restricted to the Dobby Blueprint owner. They lend the
# existing Spirit/Lilly input pair through bounded serialized operations; older
# v2 artifacts import none of these symbols and retain their prior authority.
# The UI4 context-menu register and event-take calls are strictly additive.
# Ownership of a frame's menu belongs to the frame: a window registers its rows
# once, UI4 raises them when a secondary click lands on that window, and the
# outcome is taken through the same take-call shape as the pointer, keyboard,
# pan, and resize events. The input broker keeps reporting only where the click
# landed; it owns no menu. The kernel desktop menu is correspondingly narrowed
# to clicks which hit no frame, plus frames which registered nothing, so the two
# are cleanly split. UI4 keeps menu rendering, hit testing, and teardown, and no
# application-owned slot-4 surface is introduced. The locked pointer-event
# record is unchanged and older v2 artifacts import neither symbol.
# The image-source info/read calls are strictly additive. They copy bounded
# kernel-owned image bytes into a Blueprint and do not expose kernel pointers;
# older v2 artifacts import neither symbol.
# The UI4 frame hit-testability call is strictly additive. It lets a Blueprint
# exclude one frame from cursor selection without changing frame geometry,
# composition, or the behavior of existing UI4 clients.
# Thirteen legacy declarations which never had kernel exports are intentionally
# removed from both SDK copies: four old gfx texture calls, four raw input
# queue/injection calls, and five shell1/shell-history/registry/line calls.
# No runnable artifact could resolve these names. Blueprint line output remains
# live through trueos_cabi_shell_attached_write; supported input and Shell2
# frontend contracts are unchanged.
43457378f891fd9097041cd63db2fadbf0f8d36866078298f6255fe3fe1a010d
